<LayoutDefault
  image="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-visual-sensors.jpg"
  imageAlt="grgaGrow garden monitoring demo with illustrated root zones, soil moisture and temperature readings"
  imageWidth={1265}
  imageHeight={712}
  title="grgaGrow — When does my lawn actually need water? — Gordan Grgić"
  description="Two probes, one ESP32 and the first readings sent to the cloud. A soil-monitoring project for my 49 m² lawn."
>
  <div class="lawn-notes">
    <header class="lawn-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow"><strong>grgaGrow</strong> / soil monitoring</p>
      <h1>When does my lawn actually need water?</h1>
      <p class="lede">I want to see how quickly the soil on my 49 m² lawn dries out in the sun and in the shade.</p>
      <p class="body-copy">
        On the workbench, two probes now share one controller. The first readings have reached the cloud over Wi-Fi.
      </p>
      <ProbeReadingCards />
      <div class="project-actions">
        <a class="demo-link" href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >Explore the demo ↗</a
        >
        <a
          class="demo-link live-link"
          href="/demos/my-garden/?public=grgagrow"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer">View Live garden ↗</a
        >
        <a href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=live">Owner sign-in →</a>
        <a href="#lawn-hardware">See the bench tests ↓</a>
        <a href="/projects/">← All project notes</a>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Demo: simulated readings. Public Live: the garden context, weather and sun/shade the owner shares; the bench
        samples are tests, not a live feed from the lawn. Both open without an account. Owner sign-in opens your private
        workspace.
      </p>
    </header>

    <nav class="section-nav" aria-label="Sections">
      <a href="#lawn-software">Garden app</a>
      <a href="#lawn-hardware">Probe trials</a>
      <a href="#lawn-reliability">Checking a reading</a>
      <a href="#lawn-next">Next trial</a>
    </nav>

    <ProjectOverview
      id="grgagrow"
      items={[
        {
          label: 'Today',
          text: 'Cloud app online. On the bench, both probes responded on one RS485 bus and the first samples reached the cloud with an acknowledgement. Calibration and continuous operation on the lawn remain unverified.',
        },
        {
          label: 'My role',
          text: 'Defining the garden workflow and expected behaviour, carrying out bench checks and interpreting the results.',
        },
        {
          label: 'Example',
          text: 'A probe response changed after wetting the same soil. That shows a response to wetting, not calibrated moisture accuracy.',
        },
        {
          label: 'Next step',
          text: 'A longer trial on the real lawn: compare both probes in the same moist soil, then follow how sunny and shaded areas dry.',
        },
      ]}
    />

    <figure class="dashboard-figure">
      <a
        href="/demos/my-garden/?mode=demo"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        aria-label="Open the grgaGrow simulated demo in a new tab"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-visual-sensors.jpg"
          alt="grgaGrow Demo showing two visual soil sensor cards with grass and root illustrations, simulated moisture and temperature readings, and a Visual or Compact switch"
          width="1265"
          height="712"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        THE VISUAL INTERFACE / grgaGrow, 30 October 2026. Demo readings are simulated; grass and roots are illustrative.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-software">
      <p class="folio-label">01 / SOFTWARE YOU CAN EXPLORE</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-software">One garden. Three ways to explore it.</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Demo</strong> follows the published garden setup with simulated soil readings and example activities.
        <strong>Public Live</strong> shows the measurements and garden context the owner chooses to share. Both are read-only
        and open without an account. The <strong>private owner workspace</strong> adds settings, longer history, the care
        diary, alerts and sensor setup.
      </p>
      <p>
        Switch between <strong>Visual</strong> sensor cards with grass and roots and a <strong>Compact</strong> view of the
        readings in both Demo and public Live. The illustration adds context; it does not measure root growth or indicate
        soil conditions when readings are unavailable.
      </p>
      <div class="feature-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">HISTORY & WEEKLY REPORTS</span>
          <h3>A week beneath the surface.</h3>
          <p>
            Follow seven days of moisture history and a report of hourly averages, temperature and missing intervals.
            The private workspace adds up to 30 days of history, metric and sensor filters, and CSV export.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">REGIONAL WEATHER</span>
          <h3>Weather above. Soil below.</h3>
          <p>
            Open-Meteo forecasts put air temperature, expected rainfall and precipitation chance beside the soil data.
            Forecasts use an approximate public location and remain clearly separate from measured soil conditions.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">SUN PATH & BUILDING SHADE</span>
          <h3>Follow the light.</h3>
          <p>
            A compact sun path and daylight playback move together with the garden model. Explore sun direction,
            elevation and estimated building shade. Trees, clouds and surrounding obstacles are not fully represented.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">PRIVATE / GARDEN & CARE</span>
          <h3>A diary beside the graph.</h3>
          <p>
            Save garden zones and sensor positions, then log watering, observed rain and completed mowing alongside
            moisture changes. Scheduled mowing stays separate until completion is recorded. Demo uses fictional events;
            the owner’s diary stays private.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">PRIVATE / ALERTS & HISTORY</span>
          <h3>From an alert to the evidence.</h3>
          <p>
            An alert links to the matching sensor history. Generic test push delivery to a locked Samsung S25 Ultra and
            opening Alerts were confirmed in Chrome and Samsung Internet. A push triggered by actual dry soil remains
            unverified.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="small-label">PRIVATE / SENSOR SETUP</span>
          <h3>Ready for the first probes.</h3>
          <p>
            A guide records installation checks and an estimated field-capacity reference for each sensor. System status
            separates controller contact from received readings. Saving a soil reference does not automatically validate
            calibration or watering thresholds.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="software-gallery" aria-label="Current grgaGrow application screenshots">
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-weekly-report.jpg"
            alt="grgaGrow seven-day report with hourly moisture and temperature summaries, missing-interval counts and clearly labelled example activities"
            width="1265"
            height="712"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            data-group="grgagrow-software"
            data-title="Seven-day report — simulated soil readings and example activities."
            data-index="0"
            tabindex="0"
            role="button"
            aria-label="Enlarge the seven-day report screenshot"
          />
          <figcaption>SEVEN-DAY REPORT / Simulated readings and example activities. Click to enlarge.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/grgagrow-sun-shade.jpg"
            alt="grgaGrow compact sun path and garden model showing calculated sun direction and provisional building shade"
            width="1265"
            height="712"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            data-group="grgagrow-software"
            data-title="Sun path and garden shade — a calculated model, not measured sunshine."
            data-index="1"
            tabindex="0"
            role="button"
            aria-label="Enlarge the sun and shade screenshot"
          />
          <figcaption>
            SUN & SHADE / Calculated sun position and a provisional shade model. Click to enlarge.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="stack">React · TypeScript · Vite · Supabase / PostgreSQL · Open-Meteo · SunCalc</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-hardware">
      <p class="folio-label">02 / HARDWARE — FROM WATER TO THE CLOUD</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-hardware">From a glass of water to the cloud.</h2>
      <p>
        The TH and THP probes first returned valid Modbus responses in separate trials with the Waveshare
        ESP32-S3-RS485-CAN Rev 2.2. Each then received its own address, both responded together on one RS485 bus, and
        the controller sent the first samples to the cloud over Wi-Fi.
      </p>
      <dl class="hardware-progress" aria-label="Hardware progress as of 21 November 2026">
        <div><dt>Bench result</dt><dd>TH and THP tested together</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Next milestone</dt><dd>Longer trial on the lawn</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Field status</dt><dd>Not yet installed</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <h3 id="lawn-bench">First connections on the bench.</h3>
      <div class="bench-story" aria-label="Photographs from the first grgaGrow bench tests" data-gallery-scope>
        <div class="bench-grid">
          <figure class="first-bench">
            <img
              src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/bench-setup-front.webp"
              alt="Edited photograph of the first grgaGrow bench setup with an ESP32 controller, separate supply and one TH probe in water"
              width="1536"
              height="1024"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              data-group="grgagrow-bench"
              data-index="0"
              data-title="First bench setup — one controller and one TH probe during a water test. Edited photograph."
              tabindex="0"
              role="button"
              aria-label="Enlarge the bench setup photograph"
            />
            <figcaption>First bench test with one TH probe in water.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ol class="milestones">
            <li class="pot-trial">
              <div>
                <p class="milestone-date"><time datetime="2026-11-19">19 November 2026</time></p>
                <h4>From water to soil</h4>
                <p>The THP probe was tested on its own, first in water and then in moist potting soil.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="pot-illustration" aria-hidden="true">
                <img
                  src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/thp-water-and-soil.webp"
                  alt=""
                  width="900"
                  height="450"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p class="milestone-date"><time datetime="2026-11-20">20 November</time></p>
              <h4>Two probes on the same bus</h4>
              <p>
                Both probes responded while held in air during the joint bench test. Each has its own address on the
                RS485 bus.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p class="milestone-date"><time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November</time></p>
              <h4>First readings reached the cloud</h4>
              <p>
                The ESP32-S3 runs on a 12 V supply with the USB cable unplugged. It sent readings over Wi-Fi and
                received an acknowledgement.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="reading-path" role="group" aria-label="Path of a reading">
          <span>TH + THP</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>RS485</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span
            >ESP32-S3</span
          ><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Wi-Fi</span><i aria-hidden="true">→</i><span>Cloud</span>
        </div>
        <div class="detail-photos">
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/controller-current.webp"
              alt="Edited bench photograph showing the controller and a multimeter displaying 48.31 mA DC"
              width="1448"
              height="1086"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              data-group="grgagrow-bench"
              data-index="1"
              data-title="Controller current check — approximately 48.3 mA in this test, not the maximum system current. Edited photograph."
              tabindex="0"
              role="button"
              aria-label="Enlarge the current measurement photograph"
            />
            <figcaption>The multimeter showed about 48.3 mA in this test.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <figure>
            <img
              src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/controller-led.webp"
              alt="Original close-up photograph of the controller LED with a naturally out-of-focus workbench behind it"
              width="1600"
              height="1200"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              data-group="grgagrow-bench"
              data-index="2"
              data-title="Controller LED — original focus from the first bench session."
              tabindex="0"
              role="button"
              aria-label="Enlarge the controller LED photograph"
            />
            <figcaption>A closer look at the controller during the first test.</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </div>
      </div>
      <BenchResults />
      <p class="note">
        Reference photos below show component types and assembly accessories. Models, dimensions and pictured quantities
        may differ from the ordered parts.
      </p>
      {
        [hardwareGallery.slice(0, 5), hardwareGallery.slice(5)].map((items, group) => {
          const gallery = (
            <div class="hardware-gallery">
              {items.map((item, index) => (
                <figure class="hardware-photo">
                  <img
                    src={`/images/projects/smart-lawn/hardware/${item.image}`}
                    width={item.width}
                    height={item.height}
                    alt={item.alt}
                    loading="lazy"
                    decoding="async"
                    data-index={group === 0 ? index : index + 5}
                    data-group="grgagrow-hardware"
                    data-title={`${item.title} — ${item.detail}. Reference image of this component type.`}
                    tabindex="0"
                    role="button"
                    aria-label={`Enlarge ${item.title}`}
                  />
                  <figcaption>
                    <h3>{item.title}</h3>
                    <p>{item.detail}</p>
                    {'description' in item && <p class="hardware-description">{item.description}</p>}
                    <a href={item.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                      Photo: {item.vendor} ↗
                    </a>
                  </figcaption>
                </figure>
              ))}
            </div>
          )
          return group === 0 ? (
            gallery
          ) : (
            <details class="hardware-accessories">
              <summary>Assembly accessories and specifications</summary>
              <p class="note">
                The assembly kit includes UK2.5B terminals, 120 Ω / 0.5 W termination resistors, five inline 1 A fuse
                holders and 5.5 × 2.1 mm DC adapters. Fuse selection follows measured demand; termination depends on the
                final cable route and the devices.
              </p>
              <p class="note">
                The enclosure kit includes a 230 × 150 × 85 mm box, two 10 cm DIN rails, 2 m of 4 × 0.5 mm² wire and
                five PG7 glands. Cable fit, strain relief and sealing still need checking.
              </p>
              {gallery}
            </details>
          )
        })
      }
      <div class="engineering-note">
        <p class="small-label">BEFORE THE FIRST OUTDOOR READING</p>
        <h3>A response is evidence. Accuracy needs more.</h3>
        <p>
          On 17 November, separate single-query tests returned valid responses with one probe in water, dry soil and the
          same soil after wetting. The moisture register changed after wetting. Temperature and moisture units follow
          the seller manual; these checks do not establish calibration, accuracy or reliable long-term operation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both probes have since responded together on the bench, and the first samples reached the cloud with an
          acknowledgement. That confirms the bench path, not periodic operation in the garden. Cable routing,
          protection, sealed joints and suitability for burial remain installation checks.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="architecture"
        aria-label="Planned data path: two soil probes connect via RS485 and Modbus RTU to an ESP32 controller, then via Wi-Fi and HTTPS to ingestion, PostgreSQL and the authenticated My Garden app."
      >
        <div class="probe-row">
          <span>Probe 01<br /><small>Moisture + temperature</small></span><span
            >Probe 02<br /><small>Moisture + temperature + pH</small></span
          >
        </div>
