        <div class="architecture-node">ESP32-S3 controller</div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ Wi-Fi / HTTPS</p>
        <div class="architecture-node">Ingestion → PostgreSQL</div>
        <p class="flow-label">↓ Authenticated access</p>
        <div class="architecture-node app-node">grgaGrow / My Garden</div>
        <p class="note architecture-note">
          Planned full data path · Probes tested together on the bench · First samples acknowledged in the cloud
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        Hardware snapshot: 21 November 2026 · TH and THP tested together on the bench · First samples acknowledged in
        the cloud · Field installation pending.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-reliability">
      <p class="folio-label">03 / CHECKING A READING</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-reliability">How do I know I can trust a reading?</h2>
      <div class="trust-split">
        <div>
          <p>
            The probes briefly returned zeros when first powered up. The firmware now waits 30 seconds before accepting
            values. That keeps a startup zero from being mistaken for dry soil.
          </p>
          <p>
            On my Mac I can see the state of both probes, the Wi-Fi link and the upload queue in one service tool. In a
            separate test the board fetched a firmware update over Wi-Fi, verified it and restarted.
          </p>
        </div>
        <figure class="service-board">
          <img
            src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/bench/service-board-mock.png"
            alt="The grgaGrow service tool’s Board screen, with mock Wi-Fi, probe and upload status."
            width="1196"
            height="817"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
            data-group="grgagrow-service"
            data-index="0"
            data-title="The service tool on the Mac, shown with mock data."
            tabindex="0"
            role="button"
            aria-label="Enlarge the service tool screenshot with mock data"
          />
          <figcaption>MOCK DATA / The service tool on the Mac, shown with mock data.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <h3>What if a reading never arrives?</h3>
      <p>
        A reassuring number is only useful if it can be trusted. The app checks freshness and time quality, preserves
        gaps in the history, and withholds watering suggestions when readings are unreliable. A dry trend must remain
        valid for an uninterrupted 30-minute window before the current logic suggests watering.
      </p>
      <div class="engineering-note">
        <p class="small-label">DEVELOPMENT EXAMPLE / A LOST ACKNOWLEDGEMENT</p>
        <h3>Send again. Keep one reading.</h3>
        <p>
          A measurement can reach the database even when its acknowledgement never reaches the device. The local queue
          model and ingestion code are tested together: resend that measurement, keep a single database record, then
          remove only the readings that have been acknowledged.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          The production ESP32 firmware foundation has native tests, simulated-probe checks and an ESP32 build verified
          in CI. The cloud has since acknowledged the first bench samples; real flash power-loss recovery and continuous
          operation in the garden remain unverified.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="engineering-note firmware-example" aria-labelledby="firmware-example-title">
        <p class="small-label">FIRMWARE / C++17</p>
        <h3 id="firmware-example-title">A timeout stays a timeout.</h3>
        <p>
          This is an actual excerpt from the shared firmware module used by the bench diagnostic. A complete response
          goes to validation; a timeout or transport fault returns an explicit error.
        </p>
        <p class="note">modbus_rtu.cpp · readRegisters</p>
        <Code
          code={firmwareExample}
          lang="cpp"
          themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' }}
          tabindex="0"
          aria-labelledby="firmware-example-title"
          data-firmware-code="true"
        />
        <p class="note">
          The separate decoder checks CRC, address, function and frame length before exposing registers. These checks do
          not establish calibration or continuous operation.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        The cloud workspace uses Supabase authentication and owner-scoped database access. A separate public projection
        exposes only the selected garden data; exact coordinates, private diary entries, alerts and calibration notes
        stay outside it. The owner can disable publication. Demo values never become real sensor records, and Live shows
        a waiting state when no current readings are available.
      </p>
      <p>
        I define the requirements and expected behaviour, carry out bench checks and decide which results are supported
        by the evidence.
      </p>
      <p class="stack">Supabase / PostgreSQL · Access rules · Idempotent ingestion · Local automated checks</p>
      <p class="note">
        The app and cloud services are online. Calibration, continuous physical monitoring in the garden and power-loss
        recovery remain unverified. Watering thresholds remain preliminary; automatic irrigation is not active.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-site">
      <p class="folio-label">04 / THE REAL GARDEN</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-site">A practical question, close to home.</h2>
      <p>
        The idea behind grgaGrow came from a simple observation: gardens are often watered too much or too little. Water
        is precious, and climate change makes using it thoughtfully even more important. I wanted to explore how a
        small, smart and affordable monitoring system could help people understand what their soil needs and avoid
        unnecessary watering.
      </p>
      <p>
        The system can also be extended with automatic irrigation. Adding a controlled valve and a control module could
        let grgaGrow water the garden based on soil readings and weather information. This is a planned next stage,
        following reliable monitoring and hardware testing.
      </p>
      <p>
        I still water the lawn manually. Sun, shade and soil conditions can make nearby areas behave differently. I want
        to compare how quickly they dry, how the soil temperature changes, and how long water takes to reach a probe.
      </p>
      <figure class="lawn-cover">
        <img
          src="/images/projects/smart-lawn/lawn.jpg"
          alt="My 49 square metre lawn with a robotic mower, the future test site for the two soil probes"
          width="1600"
          height="1200"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
          data-group="cover"
        />
        <figcaption>
          THE TEST SITE / My 49 m² lawn. Monitoring is not installed yet; placement and cable routes must also suit the
          robotic mower.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        This is the part I enjoy: connecting the interface to something physical, making the measurements
        understandable, and checking the whole path from a sensor in the ground to a decision on the screen.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lawn-next">
      <p class="folio-label">05 / CURRENT STATE & NEXT STEPS</p>
      <h2 id="lawn-next">Measure first. Automate later.</h2>
      <ol class="roadmap">
        <li>
          <strong>Online now</strong><p>
            Public Demo and Live views, a private cloud workspace, history, weekly reports, a garden diary, alerts,
            weather and sun/shade. Software behaviour is covered by local automated checks.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Bench path confirmed</strong><p>
            TH and THP responded together on one RS485 bus, each at its own address. Running on 12 V with USB unplugged,
            the ESP32-S3 sent its first samples over Wi-Fi and received an acknowledgement. A firmware update over Wi-Fi
            was also confirmed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Longer lawn trial next</strong><p>
            First I want to compare both probes in the same moist soil. Once installed on the lawn, they will help me
            see how quickly the sunny patch dries compared with the shaded one. Calibration, continuous operation and
            watering thresholds still need to be verified.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Add automatic irrigation</strong><p>
            Only after reliable monitoring: test a valve, shut-off behaviour and watering limits. Weather and
            notifications are available in the app; automatic irrigation remains future work.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="closing-demo">
        <div>
          <h3>Take a look beneath the surface.</h3><p>View the public garden, or explore the simulated demo.</p>
